# Selling Tone

Applies to every string a customer reads: bespoke page copy modules, `data/productTranslations*.ts`,
`messages/{en,ru,ar}.json`, product records in the DB, emails, and social captions. All three
languages, not just English.

We are selling the product, not examining it. The Intertek dossier is where a claim is
*verified*, never how it is *phrased*.

## Five rules

1. **Lead with what it does for the buyer.** Regulatory and lab facts are the proof underneath
   the claim, never the headline.
2. **Never cite the packaging as a source in body copy.** Exception: `fullInciNote` and
   precaution notes, where "as printed on the carton" tells the buyer the on-screen list matches
   the pack in their hand. That is a trust signal and it stays.
3. **No dossier vocabulary.** Banned in body copy: declared, assayed, batch pH, certificate of
   analysis, paperwork, test mark, released against, label rounding.
   **Never print a lot or batch code** (`WNL053`, `L1036B`). Keep the measured figure and the
   specification it sits inside, drop the code that identifies it: "5.75, inside a 4.50 to 6.50
   specification", never "5.75 on lot WNL053". A code also dates the page, because the box the
   buyer receives carries a different lot. Same for expiry: "three years unopened, with the
   expiry date on the box", never "lot WNL053 runs to December 2027".
4. **Never undercut a claim in the next clause.** No "a real but modest dose", "shows direction,
   not a guarantee", "we will not invent a number", "not a fault in the formula".
5. **Never name a contract manufacturer.** GENIC, SLC, CNF and the like stay off the page.
   DTS MG is fine everywhere: it owns GENOSYS, so it reads as heritage.

Also: no "the manufacturer says/records/specifies/describes". If the claim is good enough to
print, make it in our own voice.

## Examples

```
BAD   The registered piece. Five UV filters take it to SPF50+ PA++++, and the same formula
      carries niacinamide at 2%...
GOOD  Coverage, sun protection and skincare in one press of the puff, and Korea licenses it
      for all three at once. Five filters take it to SPF50+ PA++++...

BAD   The cleanser, toner and cream all carry a dermatological test mark on their cartons.
GOOD  Cleanser, toner and cream all dermatologically tested.

BAD   Declared at 2% in both. On the lots we hold certificates for it came back at 98.65%.
GOOD  A full 2% in both, and every batch is tested to prove it. The latest came back at 98.65%.

BAD   Its carton says it can go over make-up.
GOOD  Light enough to go straight over make-up.

BAD   Three years unopened; lot WNL053 runs to December 2027.
GOOD  Three years unopened, with the expiry date on the box.
```

## What is not self-defeating

Honest guidance that protects the buyer stays, and makes everything else believable:
SPF layering advice, downtime and peeling timelines, pregnancy referrals to a doctor, and
"look elsewhere if" lists that talk someone out of the wrong product.

Accuracy is never traded for tone. Reword a claim, never loosen it.
